UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Week 7, Lesson 4: Percentage problems', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Year 7 students develop percentage problems with explicit methods, worked numerical examples and reasonableness checks. Represent the situation, choose an appropriate procedure, calculate carefully and verify with an inverse, estimate or diagram. Catalogue alignment uses MA4-FRC-C-01; detailed content-point mapping remains pending.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nSolve two fraction/decimal/percentage items for percentage problems and convert one answer to another form.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: common denominators or place-value alignment correct; forms converted accurately.\n\nWATCH FOR\nAdding denominators or aligning decimals from the left.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nIndependently complete a substantial practice set on percentage problems: at least eight varied items (including one multi-step or contextual item), show methods, check three answers, and write a five-sentence reflection naming one strategy and one error to watch.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"Year 7 students develop percentage problems with explicit methods, worked numerical examples and reasonableness checks. Represent the situation, choose an appropriate procedure, calculate carefully and verify with an inverse, estimate or diagram. Catalogue alignment uses MA4-FRC-C-01; detailed content-point mapping remains pending.","lesson_number":4,"lesson_role":"Fluency and review","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA4-FRC-C-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Independently complete a substantial practice set on percentage problems: at least eight varied items (including one multi-step or contextual item), show methods, check three answers, and write a five-sentence reflection Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: common denominators or place-value alignment correct; forms converted accurately. Reasoning: I used percentage problems by attending to strategy rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Adding denominators or aligning decimals from the left. Correction: Strong answer: common denominators or place-value alignment correct; forms converted accurately. Why: the correction restores accurate use of percentage problems.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Adding denominators or aligning decimals from the left. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Solve one fresh problem that uses percentage problems with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Describe one likely mistake with percentage problems, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require new numbers, visible working and named places/values. / Require a real misconception, corrected working and a method reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answer: common denominators or place-value alignment correct; forms converted accurately. This matches the lesson skill for percentage problems.","options":["Strong answer: common denominators or place-value alignment correct; forms converted accurately.","Adding denominators or aligning decimals from the left.","Ignore place value and read digits from left to right as ones only.","Copy the model numbers and call that independent work."],"question":"Solve two fraction/decimal/percentage items for percentage problems and convert one answer to another form. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Adding denominators or aligning decimals from the left. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Strong answer: common denominators or place-value alignment correct; forms converted accurately.","Careful use of strategy with clear evidence.","Adding denominators or aligning decimals from the left.","Year 7 students develop percentage problems with explicit methods, worked numerical examples and reasonableness checks. Represent the situation, choose an…"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising percentage problems?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named strategy, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the strategy used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing zero as if it does not change the value."],"question":"A learner is practising percentage problems. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Mathematics answers need labelled working tied to place value or quantity.","options":["Show the places, values or steps and say why each step is valid.","Write only a final number with no working.","Change digits to look neater without checking place value.","Adding denominators or aligning decimals from the left."],"question":"Which working note best supports a mathematics answer about percentage problems?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the method; the others break it.","options":["Adding denominators or aligning decimals from the left.","Ignore place value and read digits from left to right as ones only.","Copy the model numbers and call that independent work.","Strong answer: common denominators or place-value alignment correct; forms converted accurately."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Year 7 students develop percentage problems with explicit methods, worked numerical examples and… — which statement is true?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Disagreement is resolved by checking places, operations and the question focus.","options":["Average the two answers without checking either.","Recheck places, operations and whether the question asked for the quantity you computed.","Pick the longer-looking working automatically.","Delete units so the answers appear to match."],"question":"Which error should you repair first if two methods disagree?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Transfer requires new numbers with the same method.","options":["Copy the model numbers and call that independent work.","Solve a fresh problem with different numbers and explain the method.","Retype the worked model numbers as the answer.","Change only handwriting and keep the same numbers."],"question":"Which practice choice shows transfer of percentage problems?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Useful feedback names the failed step and shows corrected working.","options":["Just add more digits until it looks longer.","Name the exact place or step that failed, then show corrected working for percentage problems.","Praise neat handwriting even when the places are wrong.","Tell the learner to skip working and only keep the final digits."],"question":"Which feedback comment is most useful after a mistake?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require new numbers, visible working and named places/values.","question":"Solve one fresh problem that uses percentage problems with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: common denominators or place-value alignment correct; forms converted accurately. Reasoning: I used percentage problems by attending to strategy rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require a real misconception, corrected working and a method reason.","question":"Describe one likely mistake with percentage problems, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Adding denominators or aligning decimals from the left. Correction: Strong answer: common denominators or place-value alignment correct; forms converted accurately. Why: the correction restores accurate use of percentage problems.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s4-mat-y7-w07-l4","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise percentage problems.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Number","success_criteria":["I can use percentage problems with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify strategy and explain why it matters.","I can practise check independently on a new prompt.","I can notice reasonableness needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Year 7 students develop percentage problems with explicit methods, worked numerical examples and reasonableness checks. Represent the situation, choose an appropriate procedure, calculate carefully and verify with an inverse, estimate or diagram. Catalogue alignment uses…","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working.","Year 7 students develop percentage problems with explicit methods, worked numerical examples and reasonableness checks. Represent the situation, choose an appropriate procedure, calculate carefully and verify with an inverse, estimate or diagram. Catalogue alignment uses MA4-FRC-C-01; detailed content-point mapping remains pending.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about percentage problems?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample for Percentage problems: 2/3 + 1/6 = 4/6 + 1/6 = 5/6, or 0.25=1/4=25%. Show equivalent forms before operating.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSolve two fraction/decimal/percentage items for percentage problems and convert one answer to another form.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: common denominators or place-value alignment correct; forms converted accurately.","explain":"Year 7 students develop percentage problems with explicit methods, worked numerical examples and reasonableness checks. Represent the situation, choose an appropriate procedure, calculate carefully and verify with an inverse, estimate or diagram. Catalogue alignment uses MA4-FRC-C-01; detailed content-point mapping remains pending.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Adding denominators or aligning decimals from the left. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Percentage problems.","caption":"Then check.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Represent</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">diagram/expression</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Calculate</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">careful steps</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example for Percentage problems: 2/3 + 1/6 = 4/6 + 1/6 = 5/6, or 0.25=1/4=25%. Show equivalent forms before operating. Then write your own working.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.\nJuno: Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Example for Percentage problems: 2/3 + 1/6 = 4/6 + 1/6 = 5/6, or 0.25=1/4=25%. Show equivalent forms before operating. Then write your own working."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task.","options":["Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about percentage problems?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample for Percentage problems: 2/3 + 1/6 = 4/6 + 1/6 = 5/6, or 0.25=1/4=25%. Show equivalent forms before operating. Look at each number, place or distance that carries the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Show the working","visual":[{"alt":"Reasonableness.","caption":"Units matter.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\