 your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the answer without repairing the method does not fix the error. Adding denominators or aligning decimals from the left. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task.","options":["A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working.","Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about percentage problems?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nYear 7 students develop percentage problems with explicit methods, worked numerical examples and reasonableness checks. Represent the situation, choose an appropriate procedure, calculate carefully and verify with an inverse, estimate or diagram. Catalogue alignment uses MA4-FRC-C-01; detailed content-point mapping remains pending. Now choose different numbers to show you can transfer the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSolve one fresh problem that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your answer.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new problem should use the same method as the model, but contain different numbers.","explain":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new numbers. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Transfer to a new problem"}],"title":"Year 7, Week 7, Lesson 4: Percentage problems","unit_number":2,"unit_title":"Fractions, decimals and percentages","week":7,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample for Percentage problems: 2/3 + 1/6 = 4/6 + 1/6 = 5/6, or 0.25=1/4=25%. Show equivalent forms before operating.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the places, values or distances used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Percentage problems.","caption":"Then check.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Represent</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">diagram/expression</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Calculate</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">careful steps</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Reasonableness.","caption":"Units matter.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"2